<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1978 · Sub-Saharan Africa</p><h1 class="sg-title">Arise, O Compatriots</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The national anthem of Nigeria from 1978 to 2024, its words put together from the best entries in a national contest.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>P. O. Aderibigbe; John A. Ilechukwu; Sota Omoigui; Eme Etim Akpan; B. A. Ogunnaike</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Benedict P. Odiase and the Nigeria Police Band</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Nigeria</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Nigeria</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>national anthem from 1 October 1978</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words drawn from entries to a national contest (1978); music by Benedict P. Odiase, who died in 2013)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arise-o-compatriots">Arise, O Compatriots</a>&quot; was the national anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> from 1 October 1978 until 2024. It was adopted in 1978 by the military government of the time and replaced the country's first anthem, &quot;Nigeria, We Hail Thee&quot;, which had been in use since 1960.</p><p>Its words were not written by a single author. They combine words and phrases taken from five of the best entries in a national contest, by P. O. Aderibigbe, John A. Ilechukwu, Dr. Sota Omoigui, Eme Etim Akpan and B. A. Ogunnaike. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> Police Band, under the direction of Benedict P. Odiase (1934–2013), set them to music.</p><p>The anthem has two verses, but usually only the first was sung; on some occasions the second was recited as &quot;The National Prayer&quot;. The national pledge, written by Felicia Adebola Adeyoyin in 1976, was recited immediately after the anthem was played.</p><p>In 2024 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> passed a bill restoring &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, We Hail Thee&quot;, and President Bola Tinubu signed it into law. On 29 May 2024 &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arise-o-compatriots">Arise, O Compatriots</a>&quot; was officially given up and the first anthem readopted.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A patriotic anthem calling the citizens to the service of their country; solemn and dedicated in mood.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so the Institute does not print them itself: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius" data-genius="13124621"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn">Show the lyrics from Genius</button><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press the button. <a href="https://genius.com/National-anthems-arise-o-compatriots-nigerias-national-anthem-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
